# Service Provider API Implementation Checklist

Status: Phases 0 through 2 complete. The approved contract, including the
low-level immutable-object mutation boundary, is recorded and frozen; the
audited namespace, identity, immutability, ordering, and weak-lifetime
foundations and process runtime lifecycle are verified, and atomic service
acquisition work may begin with Phase 3.

Source: `Docs/service-provider-api-proposal.md`

## Goal

Implement the proposed namespaced service-provider contract without creating a
second tooltip or context-menu runtime. DwarfUICore will expose immutable,
namespace-bound version 1 APIs over its existing process-wide services, enforce
runtime-generation and service-health validation, and preserve registrations
across ordinary provider construction.

The work includes the coordinated migration of DwarfUI to namespace `dwarfui`.
Migration of any other consumer plugin remains explicitly out of scope.

## Proposal analysis

- [ ] Treat this checklist as an implementation extrapolation, not approval of
      the proposal. Do not begin production changes until the public contract
      and the unresolved decisions in Phase 0 are approved.
- [ ] Preserve the current process-wide tooltip and context-menu implementations
      as the only backends; providers are ownership and compatibility layers,
      not parallel service implementations.
- [ ] Introduce lifecycle and acquisition infrastructure before provider
      facades. Current modules can construct, retire, or replace process state
      during module load, which conflicts with argument-first validation,
      atomic publication, and no-automatic-repair requirements.
- [ ] Refactor registration identity through the full pipeline. Namespaces must
      survive registration, detection, mediation, presentation, callback
      dispatch, diagnostics, update, removal, and stale-handle validation.
- [ ] Close the proposal's public-schema gap before production work. The
      proposal names versioned option, update, handle, definition, entry,
      callback-context, and discriminator types but does not define their
      complete fields, optionality, defaults, or validation semantics.
- [ ] Treat tooltip collision resolution and context-menu composition as
      backend changes. They cannot be implemented safely only in the public API
      wrapper.
- [ ] Keep package contract, installation resolution, reload behavior, and
      DwarfUI adoption as separate completion gates. Focused provider tests do
      not prove packaged or live integration.
- [ ] Preserve unrelated worktree and index state. Do not stash or commit as
      part of executing this checklist.

## Locked scope and constraints

- [ ] The public dependency entrypoint is `reqscript('dwarfuicore')`.
- [ ] The only public provider exports are
      `dwarfuicore.services.TooltipServiceProvider` and
      `dwarfuicore.services.ContextMenuServiceProvider`.
- [ ] Provider construction requires an exact positive integer contract major
      and a validated stable consumer namespace.
- [ ] DwarfUICore allocates every local registration identity. Consumers never
      supply, select, or mutate local or composite identities.
- [ ] Every `new()` call returns a distinct immutable API object bound to one
      service, contract major, namespace, and runtime generation.
- [ ] API objects are non-owning handles, not namespace leases. Do not add lease
      counting, `close()`, `release()`, or API-garbage-collection cleanup.
- [ ] Widget keys and opaque map handles retain weak individual lifetimes;
      namespace-wide cleanup occurs only through explicit consumer operations or
      DwarfUICore generation retirement.
- [ ] Physical target selection is namespace-neutral and precedes contribution
      resolution. Track stable physical target sequence separately from each
      namespace contribution's sequence.
- [ ] Closed discriminator sets use immutable numeric LuaDoc enums. Do not
      publish a consumer-visible service discriminator or string service name.
- [ ] Production modules start with `--@ module=true`, project modules use
      `reqscript()`, and reload-managed modules are registered in dependency
      order.
- [ ] Every new or modified class and method has complete LuaDoc comments. All
      public option, update, handle, definition, entry, callback-context, and
      discriminator records have complete versioned LuaDoc definitions.
- [ ] Provider APIs expose no `get_diagnostics()` method or public diagnostic
      record type. Retain private diagnostics for focused tests, reload
      validation, hook inspection, and development troubleshooting only.
- [ ] Provider construction and API methods never reload, tear down, clear
      script environments, or rescan overlays.
- [ ] Ordinary construction never replaces a singleton, manager, presenter,
      hook owner, namespace state, or healthy runtime generation.
- [ ] Independent consumer-plugin migrations are not authorized by this task.

## Phase 0 - Approve and freeze the version 1 contract

- [x] Record the starting DwarfUICore branch, revision, worktree state, package
      version, and current module-registry order.
- [x] Run and record the current complete unit, syntax, build, and package
      baselines before changing production code.
- [x] Inventory every `dfhack.dwarfuicore` process slot, module-local generation
      counter, eager singleton construction, automatic predecessor retirement,
      hook owner, and reload teardown path.
- [x] Inventory the current tooltip and context-menu widget keys, map-handle
      lifetimes, identity allocators, registration sequences, target precedence,
      open-session snapshots, diagnostics, and weak-reference behavior.
- [x] Approve the namespace grammar, 64-byte limit, and byte-for-byte
      comparison. Validate syntax only; do not add reservation, allocation,
      registration, or global claiming of namespace strings.
- [x] Amend the proposal with the complete transitive
      version 1 LuaDoc schema for both services. Cover map positions, options,
      updates, opaque handles, context-menu definitions and entries, callback
      selection context, referenced public discriminator enums, field
      optionality/defaults, validation, copying, canonical type names, and
      identity semantics. Reconcile the current callback context's raw
      registration identity with the proposal's opaque composite-identity rule.
- [x] Explicitly approve the version 1 public data schema amendment before
      production implementation begins.
- [x] Freeze the diagnostic boundary: public service health is observable
      through documented errors, while private diagnostic shapes are unversioned
      and may not leak definitions or callback references across namespaces.
- [x] Preserve weak lifetime cleanup for abandoned widget keys and opaque map
      handles in both services, including through every secondary detection
      index and callback-containing registration value.
- [x] Treat consumer reload, disable, teardown, and applicable save/map/world
      lifecycle handling as explicit `clear_namespace()` call sites. Do not
      infer generic Lua consumer unload from API reachability, environment
      clearing, overlay rescanning, map/world unload, or garbage collection.
- [x] Preserve the approved two-stage model: existing namespace-neutral
      widget/root/blocker/map targeting selects one physical target, then the
      service resolves that target's namespace contributions.
- [x] Preserve the approved immutable open-menu snapshot: definition updates
      affect the next opening, while ownership loss closes the entire current
      menu before any callback can run.
- [x] Preserve the approved provider/API prefixes, stable category tokens,
      non-stable diagnostic detail, handle-validation precedence, and ordinary
      `false` return boundaries from the public error contract.
- [x] Document the supported single-installation runtime model: package updates
      require a fresh process or complete core reload, while partial replacement
      and active script-path switching are unsupported and are not diagnosed.
- [x] Treat `dwarfuicore/tooltip/api` and
      `dwarfuicore/context_menu/api` as intentionally replaced public paths.
      Migrate DwarfUI and DwarfUICore tests, then remove both modules, their
      registry entries, and packaged files before release.
- [x] Ship no compatibility adapter, legacy namespace, inferred namespace, or
      compatibility window for the old direct APIs. Do not include independent
      plugin migrations in this work.
- [x] Require the coordinated DwarfUI migration before releasing the
      provider-bearing DwarfUICore package. Pair it with a DwarfUI minimum
      dependency on the assigned provider-bearing package version and claim no
      support for either mismatched old/new package combination.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [x] The proposal text and every decision above are approved or amended in the
      proposal before implementation begins.
- [x] Every DwarfUICore-owned transitive public type reachable through an API
      parameter, return, field, or callback is defined in the proposal and
      explicitly approved.
- [x] Baseline evidence is recorded without promoting unit or package evidence
      to live-runtime success.
- [x] The current process-state and registration data flows are mapped through
      presentation and callback dispatch, not only through public facades.

## Phase 1 - Build private namespace, identity, and immutability primitives

- [x] Add a private namespace validator implementing the approved byte-level
      grammar with focused boundary tests.
- [x] Add immutable numeric internal enums for service kind, runtime status,
      service health, and any other closed state set introduced by this work.
- [x] Add an immutable numeric internal public-error category enum and one
      exhaustive mapping from its members to the approved stable category
      tokens.
- [x] Add a process-wide monotonically increasing identity/sequence allocator
      that can produce the approved composite identity components without
      exposing internal records publicly.
- [x] Allocate stable physical widget target sequences separately from
      contribution sequences. Retain a target sequence until its last namespace
      contribution is removed; allocate a new one after complete removal and
      later re-registration.
- [x] Define immutable opaque map-registration handles whose private backing
      records include runtime generation, service kind, contract major,
      namespace, and local identity.
- [x] Store widget and map-handle registrations in weak ownership structures
      whose values and secondary indexes cannot keep their own weak keys alive.
- [x] Add shared private immutable-proxy support for provider exports, service
      API objects, and handles. Block ordinary assignment, ordinary method
      replacement, field attachment, backing-state inspection, and
      `setmetatable()` replacement. Treat `rawset()` and `debug.*` as explicitly
      unsupported low-level escape hatches while keeping authoritative backing
      state external and isolated from every other proxy.
- [x] Store API and handle backing records outside consumer-visible tables and
      expose only documented operations.
- [x] Test missing and malformed namespaces, minimum and maximum lengths,
      invalid ASCII, period rules, exact comparison, immutable behavior, and
      opaque backing storage.
- [x] Test composite identity uniqueness across services, namespaces, contract
      majors, local identities, and runtime generations.
- [x] Test that target and contribution sequences are independent and retain
      their approved idempotent update/removal behavior.
- [x] Test that every error enum member has one documented token, no token is
      duplicated, and unmapped categories cannot be rendered.
- [x] Test cyclic callback/environment graphs to prove weak widgets and map
      handles remain collectable while live API objects remain non-owning.
- [x] Replace caller-selected allocator ownership with the one dedicated
      reload-stable `dfhack.dwarfuicore.service_provider_identity` process slot.
      Store only a version and plain numeric identity/sequence counters there;
      do not store a module-class instance or add allocator fields to the Phase
      2 runtime record.
- [x] Preserve allocator monotonicity across supported module reconstruction
      and complete core-generation reload, resetting only with a fresh DFHack
      process. Validate the slot exactly and reject integer exhaustion before
      incrementing rather than permitting wraparound.
- [x] Approve the immutable-object mutation boundary: ordinary assignment and
      `setmetatable()` remain blocked; `rawset()` and `debug.*` are unsupported
      low-level escape hatches; authoritative backing remains external and
      cannot be mutated through a proxy or changes to another proxy.
- [x] Amend the proposal to record the approved immutable-object mutation
      boundary before using the proxy for public provider or API objects.
- [x] Test ordinary assignment, backing inspection, and `setmetatable()` as
      enforced failures. Test `rawset()` and applicable `debug.*` operations as
      unsupported self-sabotage that cannot reveal or mutate authoritative
      backing, another proxy, or shared backend state.
- [x] Validate every composite identity component before creating a recognized
      map handle: positive integer generation, known service kind, positive
      integer contract major, validated namespace, and positive allocated local
      identity. Reject missing and unknown fields.
- [x] Copy the complete identity into private handle backing and make internal
      inspection return copies or comparisons instead of the authoritative
      record, so retained input and returned tables cannot mutate handle
      identity.
- [x] Test malformed and incomplete handle identities plus mutation of the
      original identity input and any identity value returned to internal
      callers.
- [x] Make weak primary and secondary index mutation atomic. Reject duplicate
      live identities, reject changing a live key's identity, permit record
      replacement only for the same key and identity, and clear a secondary
      entry only when it still points to the exact key being removed.
- [x] Test same-key reidentification, duplicate-identity insertion, replacement,
      removal order, collection order, and surviving primary/secondary lookup
      coherence, including cyclic callback graphs.
- [x] Add a targeted audit regression suite covering every failure reproduced
      during the Phase 1 completion review before restoring checked status.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [x] Namespace validation and immutable object behavior are deterministic and
      independent of either service backend.
- [x] No public object exposes mutable authoritative runtime, namespace, facade,
      or identity state.

## Phase 2 - Establish process runtime state and lifecycle

- [x] Add one private process-state owner under DwarfUICore-owned keys in
      `dfhack.dwarfuicore`; correctness must not depend on retention of the root
      script environment.
- [x] Store only active generation, runtime status, per-service initialization
      markers, and cached service/facade records in the private process state.
- [x] Keep supported public contract majors in their implementing private
      provider adapters instead of a central runtime descriptor.
- [x] Do not add a runtime installation manifest, build identity,
      package-version comparison, content fingerprint, or per-module dependency
      version matrix.
- [x] Represent initialization, healthy, disabled, retiring, and retired states
      explicitly and reject acquisition from every disallowed state.
- [x] Separate service health from public contract compatibility in state,
      validation, diagnostics, and error reporting.
- [x] Refactor process-owned initializers so each is explicitly idempotent and
      publishes its state only after local construction succeeds.
- [x] Remove module-load behavior that automatically retires or replaces a
      healthy owner merely because a script environment was loaded again.
- [x] Preserve existing hook-chain safety while associating hook managers,
      registration managers, presenters, pollers, and services with the active
      runtime generation.
- [x] Add focused tests for missing, malformed, partial, wrong-generation,
      disabled, retiring, and retired runtime/service state.
- [x] Prove validation does not clear environments, invoke teardown, replace
      state, repair state, or rescan overlays.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [x] One process-owned runtime record governs generation, status,
      initialization, services, and facades without carrying package metadata.
- [x] Re-loading a module cannot silently replace a compatible healthy runtime
      owner.

## Phase 3 - Implement atomic service acquisition

- [ ] Add one shared private acquisition helper parameterized by immutable
      service kind and exact public contract major.
- [ ] Validate both constructor arguments before loading any private service
      prerequisite.
- [ ] Render every acquisition failure through the correct provider prefix and
      one of `INVALID_VERSION`, `UNSUPPORTED_VERSION`, `INVALID_NAMESPACE`,
      `SERVICE_UNHEALTHY`, `INITIALIZATION_BUSY`, or `INITIALIZATION_FAILED`.
- [ ] Add a table-driven constructor-failure matrix mapping every proposal
      rejection condition to its exact category, including invalid arguments,
      unsupported majors, malformed or disabled runtime/service state,
      reentrant initialization, missing prerequisites or implementations, and
      incomplete facades.
- [ ] Reject unsupported majors exactly; never bind a version 1 request to a
      later major or partially implemented adapter.
- [ ] Add per-generation, per-service in-progress state and reject reentrant or
      cyclic initialization with the provider-specific failure prefix.
- [ ] Validate current runtime status and any existing service record before
      initializing anything missing.
- [ ] Initialize prerequisites through the idempotent initializers from Phase 2
      and validate their internal contracts and health.
- [ ] Construct and validate a complete versioned service facade privately.
- [ ] Publish the facade cache and healthy service marker only after every check
      succeeds.
- [ ] Clear the in-progress marker on every failure while leaving no facade or
      healthy marker published, so a corrected later retry can succeed.
- [ ] Give unhealthy and initialization failures actionable non-stable detail
      indicating whether correction, process restart, or explicit development
      reload is appropriate without making the detail byte-stable.
- [ ] Reuse a compatible healthy singleton without replacing registrations,
      active intent/menu state, hooks, diagnostics, or namespace sequences.
- [ ] Return enough private acquisition metadata to construct a fresh bound API
      object without exposing the facade to consumers.
- [ ] Add failure-injection tests at every prerequisite and publication boundary
      plus corrected-retry, cross-service reuse, and repeated-acquisition tests.
- [ ] Assert exact prefixes and category tokens without treating changing
      diagnostic detail or stack text as byte-stable test output.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [ ] Acquisition is atomic at the facade-publication boundary and explicitly
      makes no unsupported claim about rolling back arbitrary Lua side effects.
- [ ] All proposal acquisition, partial-state, wrong-generation,
      unhealthy-state, reentrancy, and retry scenarios pass focused tests.

## Phase 4 - Namespace the tooltip backend

- [ ] Change widget registration keys from `widget` to `(namespace, widget)`
      while retaining weak widget lifetime behavior approved in Phase 0.
- [ ] Make same-namespace repeat registration idempotent without disturbing the
      original sequence; allow different namespaces to register the same widget
      independently.
- [ ] Create one namespace-neutral physical widget target record on the first
      contribution, preserve its target sequence across later namespace changes,
      and delete it only with the last contribution.
- [ ] Allocate map identities and handles through the shared composite identity
      contract and enforce namespace, service, contract-major, and generation
      ownership on update and removal.
- [ ] Make tooltip map updates atomic while preserving the opaque handle,
      composite identity, and original contribution sequence.
- [ ] Carry composite source identity through widget and map candidates, target
      adaptation, mediation, intent, presenter state, and private diagnostics.
- [ ] Resolve eligible tooltip collisions by greatest process-wide original
      contribution sequence only after namespace-neutral physical target
      selection, and reveal the next eligible contribution when the winner is
      removed or becomes ineligible.
- [ ] Ensure unregister and `clear_namespace()` remove only the bound tooltip
      namespace and release idle polling/presentation state only when no
      remaining namespace has demand.
- [ ] Retain private tooltip diagnostics for tests and development tooling,
      namespace their registration data, and expose none of them through the
      provider API.
- [ ] Preserve ordinary repeated construction, multiple entrypoints using one
      namespace, different consumers, and cross-service construction without
      replacing the tooltip backend.
- [ ] Add unit coverage for same-widget ownership, widget and map collisions,
      foreign handles, namespace cleanup, weak lifetime, ineligibility fallback,
      stale generations, private diagnostic isolation, absence of a public
      diagnostic surface, and runtime preservation.
- [ ] Prove adding, updating, unregistering, clearing, or collecting one
      namespace contribution does not reorder a still-registered physical
      widget against another root or change widget/blocker/map hit-testing.
- [ ] Prove collecting an API object changes no tooltip registration, polling,
      presentation, namespace, facade, or service-health state.
- [ ] Update existing tooltip unit and DwarfSpec fixtures to exercise the
      namespaced backend without weakening their pointer, render-hook,
      presentation, or cleanup assertions.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [ ] Tooltip registration, detection, mediation, presentation, cleanup, and
      private diagnostics retain namespace ownership end to end.
- [ ] Removing or invalidating one namespace cannot remove or hide the next
      eligible namespace contribution permanently.

## Phase 5 - Namespace and compose the context-menu backend

- [ ] Change widget registration keys to `(namespace, widget)` and preserve one
      namespace's original contribution sequence across replacement/update.
- [ ] Share one namespace-neutral physical widget target record across its
      contributions and preserve its target sequence until the last namespace
      contribution is removed.
- [ ] Convert map registrations to composite identities and enforce all foreign
      handle rejection dimensions before mutation.
- [ ] Make context-menu map updates atomic while preserving the opaque handle,
      composite identity, and original contribution sequence.
- [ ] Separate physical target selection from contribution collection so all
      eligible namespaces for the selected widget or exact map tile can
      contribute to one menu.
- [ ] Preserve existing reverse-render, root, blocker, and widget-over-map target
      precedence without consulting namespace spelling, namespace count, or a
      later contribution sequence.
- [ ] Order namespace contributions by process-wide original contribution
      sequence and preserve entry order within each validated definition.
- [ ] Retain each rendered entry's composite source identity and dispatch
      selection to exactly the callback and source context that contributed it.
- [ ] Snapshot the selected physical target, ordered contribution identities,
      validated definitions, entry order, presentation fields, and callback
      references when the menu opens.
- [ ] Keep the open snapshot unchanged when a live contribution is replaced or
      updated; apply its new definition on the next opening only.
- [ ] Close the entire menu synchronously when explicit unregister or
      `clear_namespace()` removes any snapshotted contribution.
- [ ] Detect weak widget or map-handle loss at pruning, render, and input
      validation points and close before selection dispatch.
- [ ] Close the entire menu when its source, owner, root, physical target, or
      any snapshotted contribution becomes invalid or ineligible; never
      dynamically prune, rebuild, or patch the visible menu.
- [ ] Revalidate the physical target and every composite contribution identity
      immediately before selection and invoke no callback after any validation
      failure.
- [ ] Ensure removing one namespace leaves every other eligible contribution,
      root-discovery demand, hook, and active service owner intact.
- [ ] Retain private context-menu diagnostics for tests and development tooling,
      namespace their registration and composition data, expose none through
      the provider API, and never retain definitions or callbacks in diagnostic
      output.
- [ ] Add unit coverage for same-widget and same-tile composition, mixed widget
      and map contributions, ordering, exact callback dispatch, foreign handles,
      namespace cleanup, multiple entrypoints, stale generations, and
      private diagnostic isolation and absence of a public diagnostic surface.
- [ ] Prove definition replacement/update leaves the open snapshot and captured
      callback unchanged but affects the next opening.
- [ ] Prove explicit unregister and namespace clearing close synchronously,
      weak ownership loss closes at the next safe validation point, and no
      removed contribution callback can execute.
- [ ] Prove one invalid snapshotted contribution closes the full menu without
      deleting other namespaces' registrations, which appear on the next open.
- [ ] Prove contribution addition, update, removal, collection, and namespace
      clearing cannot reorder a surviving physical widget target or change map
      fallback independently of ordinary target lifetime.
- [ ] Prove weak widgets and map handles are collectable even when context-menu
      definitions contain callbacks that refer into the consumer environment.
- [ ] Prove collecting an API object changes no context-menu registration,
      composition, active menu, namespace, facade, or service-health state.
- [ ] Extend component/live fixtures to prove real rendered combined menus and
      selection dispatch without regressing whole-event input ownership,
      anchoring, screen routing, or cleanup.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] Every selected menu entry can be traced to one composite registration
      identity through rendering and callback dispatch.
- [ ] Unregistering or clearing one namespace cannot remove another namespace's
      contribution or replace the process-wide backend.
- [ ] Open menus remain immutable across definition updates and close atomically
      on contribution or target invalidation before callback dispatch.

## Phase 6 - Expose providers and immutable version 1 APIs

- [ ] Define lightweight root-bound `TooltipServiceProvider` and
      `ContextMenuServiceProvider` classes without eagerly loading private
      service implementations.
- [ ] Export them through a closed read-only `services` empty proxy with private
      backing storage; reject replacement and consumer-added provider types.
- [ ] Implement `new(contractVersion, consumerNamespace)` with both arguments
      mandatory, provider-specific stable prefixes, exact major negotiation,
      namespace validation, and shared atomic acquisition.
- [ ] Return a distinct immutable namespace-bound API object on every successful
      construction, including repeated construction for the same namespace.
- [ ] Implement `get_contract_version()` and `get_namespace()` from private
      backing data and validate the active generation before every method.
- [ ] Implement every approved tooltip and context-menu version 1 method as a
      direct namespace-bound delegation with no per-call namespace parameter.
- [ ] Implement the exact approved transitive public LuaDoc schemas and reject
      malformed required fields, unknown closed discriminator values, and
      invalid callback shapes before mutation.
- [ ] Render API failures through the service API prefix and one of `STALE_API`,
      `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `FOREIGN_HANDLE`, `STALE_HANDLE`,
      or `SERVICE_UNHEALTHY`.
- [ ] Add a table-driven API-failure matrix covering every category and the
      proposal's validation precedence, with state unchanged after each raised
      error and `false` reserved for absent same-domain registrations.
- [ ] Validate API generation, handle shape, handle generation, and handle
      domain in the approved order; return `false` only for an absent or removed
      same-domain registration.
- [ ] Reject stale API objects and handles after generation retirement with the
      approved stable error category.
- [ ] Prove API objects cannot attach fields, replace methods, inspect facades,
      mutate provider exports, or operate on another namespace.
- [ ] Prove tooltip and context-menu APIs using the same namespace share no
      registration state because service kind remains part of identity.
- [ ] Prove the public APIs expose no `close()` or `release()` method and do not
      register API-object finalizers or namespace lease counts.
- [ ] Prove importing `reqscript('dwarfuicore')` does no service initialization,
      reload, teardown, environment clearing, or overlay rescan.
- [ ] Update root, provider, API, module-registry, and package-contract tests for
      the complete public surface and the absence of a global `get()` or public
      service discriminator.
- [ ] Assert the exact method set and return semantics for both APIs, including
      first-registration versus repeat/replacement `created`, absent
      same-domain `updated`/`removed`, and namespace-wide `changed` booleans;
      prove no public diagnostic method or unapproved method is exposed.
- [ ] Migrate DwarfUICore tests and fixtures away from both legacy direct API
      paths so they exercise the namespace-bound provider contract before the
      legacy modules are deleted.
- [ ] Prove constructors and methods raise ordinary Lua errors, never call
      `qerror()`, never return `nil, error`, and mutate no state after any
      validation, ownership, contract, runtime-state, or health failure.
- [ ] Prove a resolvable root with a missing private implementation reports the
      provider-specific `INITIALIZATION_FAILED` contract, while a missing root
      script remains DFHack's ordinary missing-script error.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] The canonical proposal examples work against one shared backend and exact
      version 1 APIs.
- [ ] The root and API object surfaces contain only approved immutable public
      contracts.

## Phase 7 - Make explicit development reload generation-safe

- [ ] Mark the active generation retiring and reject new acquisitions before
      destructive teardown begins.
- [ ] Retire every core runtime owner, invalidate all namespaces, APIs, handles,
      callbacks, presentations, hooks, and service facades from the old
      generation.
- [ ] Clear only DwarfUICore reload-managed script environments in the registry's
      safe order and rebuild prerequisites in dependency order.
- [ ] Rescan core presentation integrations only as part of explicit
      `dwarfuicore reload` reconstruction, never from provider construction or a
      service API method.
- [ ] Publish a new healthy generation only after complete reconstruction and
      runtime/service validation succeeds.
- [ ] On reconstruction failure, leave no retired facade or service marked
      healthy and keep every old API object stale.
- [ ] Preserve foreign hook wrappers according to the existing safe-retirement
      contracts and report any unavoidable inert trampoline in diagnostics.
- [ ] Prove constructors and API methods cannot reach reload, teardown,
      environment-clearing, or overlay-rescan operations.
- [ ] Add command and live lifecycle tests for successful reload, failed reload,
      stale objects, acquisition during retiring/initializing states, and clean
      retry after correction.
- [ ] Prove `dwarfui reload` neither invokes this operation nor changes another
      namespace or the DwarfUICore generation.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Successful reload creates one coherent new generation; failed reload
      leaves no falsely healthy runtime.
- [ ] No old API object, opaque handle, callback, hook owner, menu, tooltip
      intent, or namespace record can act on the new generation.
- [ ] Explicit `dwarfuicore reload` is the only operation permitted to globally
      retire every namespace or rescan core presentation integrations.

## Phase 8 - Migrate DwarfUI as the coordinated consumer

- [ ] Perform this phase only in an explicitly authorized DwarfUI checkout and
      record its revision, branch, worktree state, declared DwarfUICore
      dependency, and installed package identity.
- [ ] Acquire tooltip and context-menu APIs with exact contract major 1 and the
      approved consumer namespace `dwarfui` from DwarfUI-owned entrypoints.
- [ ] Route all DwarfUI tooltip and context-menu registrations through those
      bound APIs without creating another runtime or retaining direct access to
      private DwarfUICore modules.
- [ ] Remove every DwarfUI dependency on the legacy direct API paths; do not add
      a temporary adapter or assign a compatibility namespace.
- [ ] Update DwarfUI production code, focused and integration tests, mocks,
      documentation, `.luarc.json`, and package/integration tooling together so
      none retains a legacy direct API reference.
- [ ] Raise DwarfUI's minimum DwarfUICore dependency to the package version that
      first ships the provider contract; do not publish either coordinated
      package until the matching package is ready.
- [ ] Make `dwarfui reload` explicitly clear and rebuild only the `dwarfui`
      namespace through both acquired service APIs while preserving all other
      namespaces and the core generation.
- [ ] Route any DwarfUI disable, teardown, or applicable save/map/world cleanup
      through explicit namespace cleanup rather than API-object collection.
- [ ] Update DwarfUI focused tests and package/integration setup to install and
      resolve the intended packaged DwarfUICore before DwarfUI.
- [ ] Add an independent disposable test consumer namespace to prove DwarfUI
      reload isolation; do not edit or migrate a real independent plugin.
- [ ] Verify DwarfUI feature overlays, widgets, minecart integrations, and other
      existing service consumers retain their behavior through the provider
      boundary.

### Phase 8 completion gate

- [ ] DwarfUI is a normal namespace-bound consumer of the one DwarfUICore
      runtime.
- [ ] DwarfUI's production, test, mock, documentation, Lua configuration, and
      integration surfaces all use exact contract major 1 and namespace
      `dwarfui`, and its package dependency requires the provider-bearing core.
- [ ] Reloading DwarfUI clears and rebuilds only `dwarfui`; the disposable
      consumer's registrations and the DwarfUICore generation survive.

## Phase 9 - Complete documentation, registry, and package contracts

- [ ] Add every new reload-managed DwarfUICore module to
      `dwarfuicore/module_registry.lua` in dependency order and update both load
      and environment-clear order tests.
- [ ] Delete `dwarfuicore/tooltip/api` and
      `dwarfuicore/context_menu/api`, remove their module-registry entries, and
      remove their focused legacy facade tests after all coordinated consumers
      and fixtures use the providers.
- [ ] Update package-contract tests and package verification for the root,
      provider classes, private implementations, service modules,
      documentation, and focused tests required by the proposal.
- [ ] Update the README from "proposed and unimplemented" only after the public
      surface exists and its focused tests pass.
- [ ] Document provider acquisition, namespace selection, exact contract-major
      semantics, immutable API objects, ownership, collision behavior, opaque
      handles, cleanup, error transport, health, reload, and the absence of a
      public diagnostic API.
- [ ] Document all stable provider/API prefixes and category tokens, the
      non-stable status of diagnostic detail, handle-validation precedence, and
      the distinction between errors and ordinary `false` results.
- [ ] Document `pcall` as the recovery boundary for consumers that choose to
      handle constructor or API errors.
- [ ] Document that API objects are non-owning, individual widget/map
      registrations are weak, and consumer-controlled lifecycle operations must
      call `clear_namespace()` explicitly when namespace cleanup is required.
- [ ] Document immutable context-menu snapshots, next-open update behavior,
      full-menu closure on ownership loss, and pre-selection identity
      revalidation.
- [ ] Document that namespaces are ownership boundaries rather than security
      principals, explain that deliberate reuse shares state, and require one
      canonical identifier across entrypoints, saves, and releases.
- [ ] Document compatible and breaking contract changes and keep public contract
      majors distinct from package and private implementation versions.
- [ ] Document the supported single-installation model, offline package
      verification, fresh-process or explicit-core-reload update boundary, and
      unsupported partial replacement or active script-path switching.
- [ ] Publish the complete approved transitive LuaDoc schema, including callback
      context and public discriminator semantics, without exposing composite
      identities or private diagnostic records.
- [ ] Add canonical tooltip and context-menu examples using
      `reqscript('dwarfuicore')` and no per-method namespace argument.
- [ ] Build and inspect the expanded package and final archive; prove all
      required files exist and no DwarfUI feature module or unrelated consumer
      code is bundled.
- [ ] Prove neither legacy direct API path, compatibility adapter, nor invented
      compatibility namespace exists in source, registry, documentation, or the
      final package.
- [ ] Prove DwarfUI and the disposable independent test consumer resolve the
      same intended packaged DwarfUICore installation.
- [ ] Verify the release artifacts and documentation claim no compatibility for
      old DwarfUI with provider-bearing DwarfUICore or provider-consuming
      DwarfUI with an older DwarfUICore package.

### Phase 9 completion gate

- [ ] Registry order, expanded package, archive, public documentation, and
      installed resolution match the approved implementation.
- [ ] The package exposes no accidental internal module path as a public
      compatibility promise.

## Phase 10 - Verification, live acceptance, and cleanup

- [ ] Build an acceptance-evidence matrix mapping every proposal acceptance row
      to its exact focused unit, complete unit, package, live, manual, or cleanup
      evidence before running the final verification sequence.
- [ ] Run focused namespace, identity, runtime-state, acquisition, provider, tooltip,
      context-menu, reload, root, registry, and package-contract unit specs.
- [ ] Run the complete DwarfUICore unit suite.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Check-LuaSyntax.ps1 -IncludeTests`.
- [ ] Run the offline build and record it separately from live evidence.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Publish.ps1` and independently inspect expanded and archived
      package contents.
- [ ] Install the exact verified package into the configured DFHack test
      environment and start a fresh process before live acceptance.
- [ ] Run focused DwarfSpec provider scenarios for cold construction, repeated
      construction, multiple entrypoints, multiple consumers, collisions,
      context-menu composition, namespace cleanup, foreign handles, unhealthy
      state, initialization failure, and reload.
- [ ] Run existing focused tooltip and context-menu DwarfSpec regressions.
- [ ] Run the complete DwarfUICore DwarfSpec suite and record structured results
      and `cleanup_confirmed` separately.
- [ ] Run DwarfUI's focused and complete unit/integration suites against the
      verified DwarfUICore package after Phase 8 is authorized and complete.
- [ ] Manually verify tooltip winner fallback, combined context-menu ordering
      and exact callback dispatch, immutable open-menu update behavior,
      full-menu closure on contribution loss, repeated construction
      preservation, stale object rejection, DwarfUI reload isolation, and
      explicit core reload.
- [ ] Inspect hooks, screens, pollers, presenters, active menus/intents,
      registrations, namespace records, runtime state, and diagnostics after
      failure, reload, world unload, and final cleanup.
- [ ] Run `git diff --check` in every touched checkout.
- [ ] Verify no stale proposal wording, direct private-service consumer imports,
      temporary test state, generated scratch files, or unauthorized consumer
      migrations remain.
- [ ] Record focused unit, complete unit, syntax, build, package, focused live,
      complete live, DwarfUI integration, manual visual, and cleanup evidence as
      distinct results.

### Phase 10 completion gate

- [ ] Every acceptance scenario in the proposal has matching automated or
      explicitly manual evidence and no acceptance row is inferred from a
      narrower test layer.
- [ ] Final installed-runtime inspection shows one coherent healthy generation,
      one backend per service, correct namespace isolation, and no stale owned
      process state.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `reqscript('dwarfuicore').services` is a closed immutable namespace with
      exactly the approved provider classes.
- [ ] Both providers enforce exact major 1 and the approved namespace contract
      before service initialization.
- [ ] Namespace handling is syntax-only: no reserved names, allocation,
      registration, claiming, normalization, or namespace-acquisition API
      exists.
- [ ] Every construction returns a distinct immutable API object over one
      compatible healthy process-wide backend.
- [ ] API object collection never changes registrations; no namespace lease,
      reference count, `close()`, or `release()` contract exists.
- [ ] Weak widgets and map handles disappear from all primary and secondary
      registration indexes when collected, including with cyclic callback
      graphs.
- [ ] Widget and map registrations retain composite identity and namespace
      ownership through detection, presentation, callback dispatch, private
      diagnostics, update, cleanup, and stale validation.
- [ ] Local identities are allocated only by DwarfUICore, and using one
      namespace across tooltip and context-menu APIs does not merge service
      state.
- [ ] Tooltip collisions and context-menu composition follow the approved
      deterministic version 1 rules.
- [ ] Namespace-neutral physical targeting uses a stable target sequence that is
      independent from tooltip winner and context-menu contribution ordering.
- [ ] Context-menu definition updates affect the next opening, ownership or
      target invalidation closes the immutable current menu, and no invalidated
      callback can execute.
- [ ] Foreign handles and stale APIs are rejected across namespace, service,
      contract-major, and generation boundaries.
- [ ] Every public failure uses its approved prefix and category, ordinary
      absence returns `false`, and no public operation uses `qerror()` or
      `nil, error` transport.
- [ ] Provider APIs expose no diagnostic method or record type; private
      diagnostic state remains usable by focused tests and development tooling
      without leaking definitions or callback references.
- [ ] Both APIs expose exactly the approved methods and boolean/handle return
      semantics, and every transitively reachable DwarfUICore-owned public data
      type matches the explicitly approved LuaDoc schema.
- [ ] Ordinary construction preserves singleton owners, registrations, active
      presentation state, hooks, health, diagnostics, and sequences.
- [ ] Partial, malformed, wrong-generation, disabled, retired, and reentrant
      states fail clearly and
      are never automatically repaired or replaced.
- [ ] Explicit core reload is generation-safe, and DwarfUI reload affects only
      namespace `dwarfui`.
- [ ] Consumer reload, disable, teardown, and applicable lifecycle cleanup use
      explicit namespace clearing rather than inferred Lua unload or API-object
      garbage collection.
- [ ] DwarfUI consumes both services through the approved provider boundary;
      no independent plugin has been migrated by this task.
- [ ] The coordinated DwarfUI release updates every legacy reference and
      requires the provider-bearing DwarfUICore package version; neither
      mismatched old/new pairing is presented as supported.
- [ ] Both legacy direct API modules and their registry/package entries are
      absent, with no compatibility adapter or legacy namespace shipped.
- [ ] Module registry, package contents, installed resolution, documentation,
      and public LuaDoc match the approved contract.
- [ ] All new and modified classes and methods have LuaDoc comments.
- [ ] Focused, complete, syntax, build, package, live, visual, and cleanup gates
      pass and are reported without conflation.

